From acf5dc3949b641ff6d453b07b352b8705c5e798c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ashish Kumar Singh <ashishkmr472@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:50:43 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] [XLA:CPU] A deserialized thunk sequence keeps one
 communicator across its nested sequences

The CPU thunk emitter gives every collective of a program one communicator resource, those in a while loop's body,
a call's and a conditional's branches included, and a while, call or conditional thunk uses its nested sequences'
resources, so the execution graph orders a loop against the collectives beside it. ThunkSequenceSerDesProtobuf
deserialized each nested sequence's resources apart (WhileThunkFromProto, CallThunkFromProto and
ConditionalThunkFromProto each built a fresh serdes, and the resources the enclosing sequence listed for the nested
thunk went unused): after a roundtrip a loop no longer shared its body's communicator with the collectives beside
it, the graph lost those edges, and ranks entered the loop's all-reduce and an all-gather beside the loop in
different orders, their rendezvous timing out.

A serialized communicator now carries an id unique across the sequence tree (ResourceUsersProto.resource_id). Each
top-level ToProto or FromProto builds its map (communicator to id, or id to communicator) on the stack and passes it
by reference through the nested sequences, so a communicator shared across them deserializes once; communicators of
different ids stay apart. Tokens keep the encoding without ids (infeed and outfeed thunks recreate theirs from their
own protos), and a proto without ids deserializes as before. A sequence's resource rows now follow its first use of
each resource, so its serialized bytes no longer depend on resource addresses. The public serdes interface is
unchanged.
